The mosquito that transmits Zika is everywhere on the continent.

00:41:17.157 --> 00:41:19.919
That is why the solutions being considered are radical.

00:41:27.225 --> 00:41:28.506
In the city of Piricicaba,

00:41:28.566 --> 00:41:29.107
in Brazil...

00:41:29.787 --> 00:41:32.189
A new weapon against mosquitoes is being tested.

00:41:33.611 --> 00:41:34.031
A day,

00:41:34.612 --> 00:41:36.393
It could be deployed worldwide.

00:41:40.777 --> 00:41:41.918
We're marking the locations here.

00:41:42.639 --> 00:41:44.020
and each time we get closer to a point,

00:41:44.641 --> 00:41:44.921
It beeps.

00:41:45.642 --> 00:41:46.562
It's time to set them free.

00:41:52.027 --> 00:41:54.550
This man is not spraying insecticides.

00:41:56.483 --> 00:42:01.186
They release tens of thousands of mosquitoes.

00:42:04.168 --> 00:42:06.089
They are released in this area three times a week.

00:42:07.549 --> 00:42:08.050
And each time,

00:42:09.050 --> 00:42:10.971
That's roughly 250,000 mosquitoes.

00:42:14.453 --> 00:42:16.094
These mosquitoes are not ordinary.

00:42:21.297 --> 00:42:22.838
They are genetically modified.

00:42:25.735 --> 00:42:26.735
And they are on a mission.

00:42:33.257 --> 00:42:34.537
This mission begins here.

00:42:40.859 --> 00:42:42.880
in the world's largest mosquito factory.

00:42:49.662 --> 00:42:50.322
Each week,

00:42:50.822 --> 00:42:52.642
100 million insects are produced there.

00:42:57.683 --> 00:42:59.884
Carla Tepedino is overseeing the operations.

00:43:00.884 --> 00:43:02.745
And she doesn't hold mosquitoes in high regard.

00:43:05.785 --> 00:43:07.086
No matter where you are on the planet,

00:43:07.546 --> 00:43:09.567
Mosquitoes are the cause of many problems.

00:43:10.127 --> 00:43:10.607
To tell the truth,

00:43:11.087 --> 00:43:15.168
It would probably be a good idea for humanity to completely eradicate this insect.

00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:23.631
It took 30 years for science to develop the technology at the heart of this factory.

00:43:26.087 --> 00:43:28.416
This is a very ingenious process taking place here.

00:43:30.855 --> 00:43:34.638
A specific gene is inserted inside a mosquito egg.

00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:38.762
This gene forces the larvae to self-destruct.

00:43:40.363 --> 00:43:45.428
And this time bomb is contained in each of the eggs that these millions of mosquitoes will produce.

00:43:47.690 --> 00:43:51.833
Here we have our dried eggs and the factory produces almost 1000 grams per week.

00:43:54.631 --> 00:44:00.417
But how can these mosquitoes reach adulthood if they are born with a gene that destroys them in the larval stage?

00:44:06.463 --> 00:44:11.848
The water in which the larvae develop is infused with an antidote that prevents the gene from being triggered.

00:44:13.970 --> 00:44:15.892
As long as the mosquitoes remain in the factory,

00:44:16.513 --> 00:44:17.754
They are developing normally.

00:44:19.299 --> 00:44:20.119
After a few days,

00:44:20.760 --> 00:44:22.660
The larvae are separated according to their sex.

00:44:25.481 --> 00:44:26.381
The male is smaller.

00:44:26.781 --> 00:44:27.902
The female is larger.

00:44:28.482 --> 00:44:30.703
Therefore, using a filtration process,

00:44:30.743 --> 00:44:32.883
I can sort the males that pass through this filter.

00:44:33.344 --> 00:44:34.844
while the females remain trapped.

00:44:38.845 --> 00:44:40.686
The females remain at the factory to lay their eggs.

00:44:42.587 --> 00:44:43.487
And they need to be fed.

00:44:45.271 --> 00:44:48.594
A production line operating day and night is required.

00:44:50.656 --> 00:44:53.418
They need 20 liters of blood per day to feed themselves.

00:44:55.840 --> 00:44:56.320
The males,

00:44:56.640 --> 00:44:56.781
them,

00:44:57.421 --> 00:44:58.502
have a different destiny.

00:45:01.184 --> 00:45:05.748
The male is released into the wild and all he has to do is find the females and reproduce with them.

00:45:06.729 --> 00:45:08.890
This is the primary role of the male.

00:45:10.692 --> 00:45:13.734
They will be released into the wild in search of a female.

00:45:14.595 --> 00:45:15.595
They will then mate.

00:45:20.457 --> 00:45:22.297
But the larvae will not be able to survive.

00:45:23.218 --> 00:45:26.079
The male transmits his deadly gene to his offspring.

00:45:27.959 --> 00:45:28.759
In a few days,

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:30.740
the gene begins to be expressed.

00:45:32.941 --> 00:45:36.682
And since the antidote used in the laboratory cannot be found in nature,

00:45:37.382 --> 00:45:38.382
The larvae die.

00:45:44.491 --> 00:45:45.571
Deprived of offspring,

00:45:45.751 --> 00:45:48.272
The mosquito population is declining rapidly.

00:45:48.892 --> 00:45:50.973
but the process must be repeated constantly.

00:45:59.795 --> 00:46:04.417
Genetic modification is not the only scientific solution against mosquitoes.

00:46:05.317 --> 00:46:09.598
Trials are underway to test the effectiveness of a bacterium called Volbachia,

00:46:10.178 --> 00:46:12.339
capable of paralyzing mosquito larvae.

00:46:14.611 --> 00:46:17.412
The future of each approach depends greatly on its cost.

00:46:20.093 --> 00:46:23.535
Many fear that it will be too expensive for those who need it most.

00:46:24.235 --> 00:46:25.535
developing countries.

00:46:28.237 --> 00:46:35.339
We must remain very vigilant to ensure that these control methods are affordable for the populations where they will be used.

00:46:35.680 --> 00:46:36.840
and especially in the long term.

00:46:37.240 --> 00:46:39.401
Will this decrease be permanent?

00:46:39.961 --> 00:46:44.083
This is one of the major challenges of these new approaches being proposed for mosquito population control.

00:46:45.299 --> 00:46:48.600
No one disputes the need to take strong action against the mosquito.

00:46:50.201 --> 00:46:52.862
But the Zika story is far from over.

00:46:54.923 --> 00:46:58.265
Even though Zika cases have decreased significantly in South America,

00:46:58.965 --> 00:47:02.406
Most experts fear that they will reappear in the coming years.

00:47:04.187 --> 00:47:05.207
And on the Brazilian side,

00:47:05.928 --> 00:47:08.949
The conclusions of the latest research are even more alarming.

00:47:12.331 --> 00:47:13.191
Dr. Brasile...

00:47:13.827 --> 00:47:16.288
closely monitors the evolution of the disease across the country.

00:47:19.549 --> 00:47:22.910
As labor progressed and the children were born,

00:47:23.610 --> 00:47:27.551
We understood that microcephaly was only the tip of the iceberg.

00:47:28.611 --> 00:47:36.354
Recent research suggests that many infants born without microcephaly can still develop neurological problems later in life.

00:47:38.294 --> 00:47:41.035
There are babies who are born with a normal-sized skull.

00:47:41.949 --> 00:47:44.271
But in whom microcephaly develops later.

00:47:45.112 --> 00:47:47.434
She then presents with vision deficits,

00:47:48.455 --> 00:47:49.555
